<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dogras</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dogra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dogras are a people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> region in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Dogra brothers, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhian-singh">Dhian Singh</a>, rose to great power at the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>'s death in 1839 their factional manoeuvres contributed to the instability that led to the Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> wars. Gulab Singh afterwards became ruler of Jammu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fakir Azizuddin, a Muslim, served as Ranjit Singh's principal foreign minister and envoy to the British; the <b>Dogra</b> Dhian Singh rose to become chief minister; and finance was managed successively by officials such as Diwan Bhawani Das, Diwan Ganga Ram and Diwan Dina Nath.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 19th century Basohli fell under the Sikh Empire and was granted to the <b>Dogra</b> rulers of Jammu, who absorbed it in 1836.</blockquote><a href="/state/basohli-state">Basohli State</a></li><li><blockquote>Gulab Singh, a <b>Dogra</b> noble who had served Ranjit Singh and became Raja of Jammu, extended his rule to Ladakh.</blockquote><a href="/state/jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state">Jammu and Kashmir (princely state)</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 19th century Jasrota was absorbed by the <b>Dogras</b> under the Sikh Empire, and it became part of Jammu and Kashmir (princely state) in 1846.</blockquote><a href="/state/jasrota-state">Jasrota State</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1834 and 1842 the <b>Dogra</b> general Zorawar Singh conquered Ladakh for Gulab Singh, and it became part of the princely state of Jammu and Kashmir.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ladakh">Kingdom of Ladakh</a></li><li><blockquote>Gulab Singh, <b>Dogra</b> ruler of Jammu under the Sikh Empire, conquered Kishtwar in 1821.</blockquote><a href="/state/kishtwar-kingdom">Kishtwar Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
